{"step_index":37,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T05:31:10Z","input_tokens":20517,"cache_read_tokens":121171,"output_tokens":5449,"thinking":"Initial hypotheses were confirmed and critical bugs were identified. Now reviewing findings to create a concise, actionable audit report. Need to ensure the report is razor-sharp and saves time.\n\n\nCritical issues identified in scene geometry and shaders. Track-crossing obstacles, specifically `V5_HedgeRing` and `V5_Hangar_5`, intersect the race track; adjustments to minimum radii and placement filters are required. Also, observed an over-range albedo bug on grandstand flags.\n\n\nThe albedo bug on the grandstand flags stems from an incorrect color space conversion: raw 8-bit RGB values are being directly mapped to linear color, resulting in excessively bright reflections. Normalizing the color values to the 0-1 range, or applying a sRGB-to-linear conversion, should resolve the blowout effect.\n\nA material conflict exists where `Mat_Neon_Orange` is shared across three unrelated meshes, causing unintended visual behaviors when animating a single mesh's emission strength. The fix involves assigning a dedicated material to the FIA rain light to isolate its animation from the other meshes that share the same material.\n\n\nObserved sky dome banding in low-horizon shots, quantified at up to 14.14 luminance steps per scanline. Increasing dome segments or applying a smooth shader gradient with a warm solar azimuth glow eliminates the banding artifacts. High-altitude shots appear darker due to the low specular of the ground materials; increasing the richness of the grass and runoff improves aerial readability while maintaining low specular for road/line/kerb.\n\n\nGood progress: all scene Empties are correctly organized. The `RIG_F1_Race` object should be moved to the appropriate collection to further improve organization. Future naming conventions for new objects should ensure they are appropriately categorized. The brake glow effect is already implemented.\n\n\nVerifying the brake glow effect's active drivers and visibility is crucial; potential material masking by brake disc or duct geometries requires immediate confirmation. Examining the `Mat_Brake_HeatGlow` driver variables and related mesh visibilities and scales is essential. The analysis focuses on verifying the integrity of the heat glow effect within Blender, ensuring proper functionality before proceeding.\n\n\nInitial material checks have confirmed flat reflections and roughness settings on key components like halo fins and endplates; titanium components require further review to verify the correct material assignment. Tire materials and settings have been confirmed for the front and rear tires. Found the steering wheel bone's driver has an euler rotation based on steering and a ratio. Critical observation: steering wheel driver is confirmed, but the camera position relative to the cockpit needs verification.\n\n\nThe `Bone_Steering_Wheel` position observation reveals that the onboard cameras are positioned far forward of the steering wheel and cockpit; neither `S02_Onboard` nor `S19_Onboard3` is a T-cam. These positions are inconsistent with a true F1 cockpit camera. Correct positioning requires shifting the camera view to around `-0.30` meters forward relative to the cockpit, to see the steering wheel's rotation.\n\n\nThe brake glow effect requires thorough investigation to pinpoint driver variable targets within `Mat_Brake_HeatGlow` and correlate them with the bounds of `GEO_F1_BrakeGlow_*` meshes against possible occlusions by `GEO_F1_BrakeDisc_*` and `GEO_F1_BrakeDuct_*` geometries. Precise frame-by-frame occlusion data is needed for `Cam_S20_StoweTele` and any other relevant shots at 1-frame resolution. Determining the rest-space coordinates of the steering wheel, halo hoop, camera anchor, front wheels, and nosecone is essential for accurate T-cam and cockpit onboard camera placement. Crucially, a significant error was discovered: `V5_CarLightReceivers` contains only decal patches, not the car body meshes, which were erroneously excluded by a conditional in `v5_anim.py`.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_file","args":{"Content":"\"# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\\nimport bpy, os\\nfrom mathutils import Vector\\n\\nPROJ = r\\\"D:\\\\项目session\\\\blender-supercar\\\"\\nbpy.ops.wm.open_mainfile(filepath=os.path.join(PROJ, \\\"LATEST\\\", \\\"v5_stage_anim.blend\\\"))\\nscene = bpy.context.scene\\nrig = bpy.data.objects[\\\"RIG_F1_Race\\\"]\\n\\n# 1. Check Mat_Brake_HeatGlow driver and GEO_F1_BrakeGlow_FL geometry at a heavy braking frame\\nm_bg = bpy.data.materials.get(\\\"Mat_Brake_HeatGlow\\\")\\nif m_bg and m_bg.node_tree and m_bg.node_tree.animation_data:\\n    for d in m_bg.node_tree.animation_data.drivers:\\n        print(\\\"BrakeGlow DRV:\\\", d.data_path, \\\"expr=\\\", d.driver.expression, \\\"is_valid=\\\", d.is_valid)\\n        for v in d.driver.variables:\\n            t = v.targets[0]\\n            print(\\\"  var:\\\", v.name, \\\"id_type=\\\", t.id_type, \\\"id=\\\", t.id.name if t.id else None, \\\"path=\\\", t.data_path)\\n\\n# Find frame with max brake_heat\\nmax_bh = (-1.0, 1)\\nfor f in range(1, 2162, 5):\\n    scene.frame_set(f)\\n    bh = rig.get(\\\"brake_heat\\\", 0.0)\\n    if bh > max_bh[0]:\\n        max_bh = (bh, f)\\nprint(\\\"Max brake_heat sampled:\\\", max_bh)\\n\\nscene.frame_set(max_bh[1])\\ndg = bpy.context.evaluated_depsgraph_get()\\nm_bg_eval = m_bg.evaluated_get(dg)\\nfor n in m_bg_eval.node_tree.nodes:\\n    if n.type == 'EMISSION':\\n        print(\\\"At max brake_heat frame, evaluated Mat_Brake_HeatGlow strength =\\\", n.inputs[1].default_value)\\n\\nfor nm in (\\\"GEO_F1_BrakeGlow_FL\\\", \\\"GEO_F1_BrakeDisc_FL\\\", \\\"GEO_F1_BrakeCaliper_FL\\\", \\\"GEO_F1_BrakeDuct_FL\\\", \\\"GEO_F1_RimSpokes_FL\\\"):\\n    ob = bpy.data.objects.get(nm)\\n    if ob:\\n        bb = [Vector(c) for c in ob.bound_box]\\n        mn = Vector((min(v.x for v in bb), min(v.y for v in bb), min(v.z for v in bb)))\\n        mx = Vector((max(v.x for v in bb), max(v.y for v in bb), max(v.z for v in bb)))\\n        print(f\\\"  {nm:<26} hide_render={ob.hide_render} verts={len(ob.data.vertices)} local_min={[round(x,3) for x in mn]} local_max={[round(x,3) for x in mx]}\\\")\\n\\n# 2. Check cockpit / steering wheel / halo / T-cam bounds in armature space (fram\n<truncated 2969 bytes>","Overwrite":"true","Path":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\86052\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\brain\\\\1a8ac0b5-f848-4c0e-bc45-8abba2abb2e8\\\\scratch\\\\audit_round1_detail.py\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
